Description: SSSD Smart Card and PIV certificate authentication manager
 Generates, validates and deploys the SSSD configuration needed to log in to
 Linux with an X.509 certificate held on a Smart Card or PIV token.
 .
 Its central operation is to read a real certificate, work out which of the
 certificate-to-user mapping mechanisms that certificate can actually support,
 rank them with the reason for each score, and emit a complete profile together
 with the sssd.conf certmap section to match. The issuing PKI is irrelevant:
 Microsoft AD CS, EJBCA, Blinky and any other X.509 PKI all feed the same
 mechanisms, and only the fields present in the certificate differ.
 .
 It is built for the case where the Linux host is a member of one Active
 Directory domain while the user accounts live in a trusted one. It refuses to
 configure certificate mapping until identity lookup through that trust already
 works, because a certmap rule cannot repair a broken trust.
 .
 The included guided setup script is distribution aware, using authselect on the
 Red Hat family and pam-auth-update on Ubuntu rather than printing one command
 for everybody. It takes a backup before every change, rolls back on failure,
 and never edits the PAM stack unless explicitly asked.
 .
 The private key on the card is never read and no PIN is ever stored or
 forwarded. Every option written to sssd.conf traces to current upstream
 documentation; see the reference under /usr/share/doc/sssd-smartcard.
